						<mx:Label text=".url"/>
					</mx:HBox>
					
					<mx:CheckBox id="separate_dvd_folder_format" label="Separate DVD Folder Formats?" change="config.separateDVDFolderFormat = separate_dvd_folder_format.selected;" selected="{config.separateDVDFolderFormat}" color="#000000" toolTip="If you would like to use a different format when renaming DVD folders, check this box and make any needed adjustments."/>
					<mx:Label text="DVD Movie Folder Format ({tooltipConfig.renameFormatDVDFolder})" enabled="{separate_dvd_folder_format.selected}" selectable="true"/>
					<mx:TextInput id="dvd_movie_format" text="{config.renameFormatDVDFolder}" change="{config.renameFormatDVDFolder = dvd_movie_format.text}" width="100%" enabled="{separate_dvd_folder_format.selected}"/>
					<mx:Label text="DVD Movie Folder With AKA Format ({tooltipConfig.renameAKAFormatDVDFolder})" enabled="{separate_dvd_folder_format.selected}" selectable="true"/>
					<mx:TextInput id="dvd_movie_with_aka_format" text="{config.renameAKAFormatDVDFolder}" change="{config.renameAKAFormatDVDFolder = dvd_movie_with_aka_format.text}" width="100%" enabled="{separate_dvd_folder_format.selected}"/>
				</mx:VBox>
				<mx:TextArea width="50%" editable="false" height="100%">
					<mx:text>
						<![CDATA[It is highly recommended you include the IMDB <tt> in the filename itself (not just a the folder name) to help ease future renaming/updating using the Show TT? and Use TT? options in the renamer. When changing to a new renaming format for the first time, it is recommended you use the Duplicate for Testing functionality before applying to your real media files, to ensure your format is working as you expect it to.
            
The following substitutions are made in the formats to the left:
	<t> or <title> -> The movie title
	<tt> or <imdb> -> The IMDB unique 'tt01234567' number for the movie.  It is always recommended you include this as it will allow you to distinguish movies using Zeeb and other media cataloguing tools in the future.
	<a> or <aka> -> The movie Also Known As title you selected, when a foreign or secondary title is used. Note that if the AKA includes the title as a subpart, it will completely replace the original title and use the normal format.
	<r100> or <rating100> -> The IMDB movie rating as a whole number 0-100, where 6.4/10 is '64'.
	<r10> or <rating10> -> The IMDB movie rating as a decimal number 0-10, such as '6.4'.			
	<s> or <saved> -> Indicate where the saved parts are added to the filename.  Usually this would be at the end of the filename format.
	<y> or <year> -> The year for this movie.
	<d> or <directors> -> The list of comma sepated directors
  <D> or <rdirectors> -> The list of comma sepated directors, last name first
	<d1> or <director> -> The first director listed
  <D1> or <rdirector> -> The first director listed, last name first
	<g> or <genres> -> The list of comma sepated genres
	<g1> or <genre> -> The first genre listed
  <o> or <original> -> Include original filename (useful for folder renames only typically)
  <c> or <mpaa> or <certification> -> Include the MPAA (or other certificate from non-US) Rating if available.
  <stars>, <star1>, <stars2>, <stars3> -> Include all stars, 1 star, up to 2 stars, or up to 3 stars in the filename. You can also set the stars separate in the Options.
  <duration> or <time> -> Include the IMDB listed duration of the movie. This may not match the actual time of your file if it has additional scenes, unrated, etc.         
  <H*> -> Imdb duration hours if greater than zero. Use like <H> for "3", <HH> for "3H", <Hh> for "3h" <H hours> for "3 hours".
  <M*> -> Imdb duration in minutes. Use like <M> for "14", <MM> if you want to "14M", <Mm> for "14m" <M mins> for "14 mins".
	Any other text will be included as is, such as spaces and punctuation (excluding file system punctuation such as colons, double quotes, question marks, and asterisks). You may use slashes to indicate that the saved file should be written to another folder. For instance, if you want to have all of your movies in a folder based on the movie title, but in that folder have your files named 'tt01234567.mkv', this would allow that.
						]]>
					</mx:text>
				</mx:TextArea>
			</mx:HBox>
			
		</mx:VBox>
		
